<html prefix="dc: http://purl.org/dc/terms/ mw: http://mediawiki.org/rdf/" about="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Redirect/revision/1380126417"><head prefix="mwr: //en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Redirect/"><meta charset="utf-8"/><link rel="dc:replaces" resource="mwr:revision/1310515518"/><meta property="mw:revisionSHA1" content="9tss4j31aw6afnritgcy0u6mp65fcs8"/><meta property="dc:modified" content="2026-10-13T23:17:40Z"/><meta property="mw:pageNamespace" content="0"/><meta property="mw:pageId" content="27227184"/><meta property="mw:htmlVersion" content="2.8.0"/><meta property="mw:html:version" content="2.8.0"/><base href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"/><link rel="dc:isVersionOf" href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disjunct_matrix"/><title>Disjunct matrix</title><meta property="mw:jsConfigVars" content='{"wgParsoidHtmlVersion":"2.8.0"}'/><meta property="mw:generalModules" content="ext.math.polyfills|ext.cite.ux-enhancements"/><meta property="mw:moduleStyles" content="ext.math.styles|ext.cite.parsoid.styles|ext.cite.styles|mediawiki.skinning.content.parsoid"/><link rel="stylesheet" href="/w/load.php?lang=en&amp;modules=ext.math.styles%7Cext.cite.parsoid.styles%7Cext.cite.styles%7Cmediawiki.skinning.content.parsoid%7Cmediawiki.skinning.interface%7Csite.styles&amp;only=styles&amp;skin=vector"/><meta http-equiv="content-language" content="en"/><meta http-equiv="vary" content="Accept"/><meta http-equiv="x-mediawiki-render-id" content="4abcd725-c75c-11f1-bba8-0970ec2baa62"/></head><body class="mw-content-ltr sitedir-ltr ltr mw-body-content mediawiki mw-parser-output parsoid-body" lang="en" dir="ltr" data-mw-parsoid-version="0.24.0.0-alpha26" data-mw-html-version="2.8.0"><section data-mw-section-id="0" id="mwAQ"><p id="mwAg">In mathematics, a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Logical_matrix" title="Logical matrix" id="mwAw">logical matrix</a> may be described as <b id="mwBA"><i id="mwBQ">d</i>-disjunct</b> and/or <b id="mwBg"><i id="mwBw">d</i>-separable</b>. These concepts play a pivotal role in the mathematical area of non-adaptive <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Group_testing" title="Group testing" id="mwCA">group testing</a>.</p>

<p id="mwCQ">In the mathematical literature, <i id="mwCg">d</i>-disjunct matrices may also be called <b id="mwCw"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Superimposed_code" title="Superimposed code" id="mwDA">superimposed codes</a></b><sup about="#mwt1" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-DeBonisVaccaro2003_1-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"DeBonisVaccaro2003"}}'><a href="./Disjunct_matrix#cite_note-DeBonisVaccaro2003-1" id="mwDQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDw">[</span>1<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEA">]</span></span></a></sup> or <b id="mwEQ"><i id="mwEg">d</i>-cover-free families</b>.<sup about="#mwt2" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ErdoesEtAl1985_2-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ErdoesEtAl1985"}}'><a href="./Disjunct_matrix#cite_note-ErdoesEtAl1985-2" id="mwEw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwFA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFQ">[</span>2<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwFw">According to Chen and Hwang (2006),<sup about="#mwt3" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ChenHwang2006_3-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ChenHwang2006"}}'><a href="./Disjunct_matrix#cite_note-ChenHwang2006-3" id="mwGA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwGQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwGg">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwGw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<ul id="mwHA"><li id="mwHQ">A matrix is said to be <i id="mwHg">d</i>-separable if no two sets of <i id="mwHw">d</i> columns have the same boolean sum.</li>
<li id="mwIA">A matrix is said to be <span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt4" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"\\overline{d}"}}' id="mwIQ"><span class="mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" class="mathjax_ignore" alttext="{\displaystyle {\overline {d}}}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0">
        <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
          <mover>
            <mi>d</mi>
            <mo accent="false">¯<!-- ¯ --></mo>
          </mover>
        </mrow>
      </mstyle>
    </mrow>
    <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\displaystyle {\overline {d}}}</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/147fdfc5e4452853cb2946348d7e2e9cdb11a6d4" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -0.338ex; width:1.335ex; height:3.009ex;" alt="{\displaystyle {\overline {d}}}"/></span>-separable (that's <i id="mwIg">d</i> with an overline) if no two sets of <i id="mwIw">d</i>-or-fewer columns have the same boolean sum.</li>
<li id="mwJA">A matrix is said to be <i id="mwJQ">d</i>-disjunct if no set of <i id="mwJg">d</i> columns has a boolean sum which is a superset of any other single column.</li></ul>

<p id="mwJw">The following relationships are "well-known":<sup about="#mwt5" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ChenHwang2006_3-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ChenHwang2006"}}'><a href="./Disjunct_matrix#cite_note-ChenHwang2006-3" id="mwKA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwKQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwKg">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwKw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

